--TEST--
`Core\Uri::decodeComponent` at its two edges: a `%` that does not begin a well-formed escape decodes to itself, and a well-formed escape for an octet no `string` can hold is answered as the `bytes` it is and refused only by the `as string` a caller wanting text writes — a lone byte, a truncated sequence, a surrogate and an overlong form alike — spec § 12, `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The neighbouring cases show this member against its encoder, over every
// ASCII byte and against `decodeFormValue`. What none of them asks is where it
// stops: `decode` reads a `%` as an escape only when two hex digits follow it,
// and it answers *octets*, which a `string` then has to be able to hold. Both
// edges are named here with the value on each side of them.
//
// The second edge moved one member along when the decoders came to answer
// `bytes` (spec § 12): the octets themselves always have an answer, and it is
// the `as string` every row below writes that refuses the ones no `string` can
// hold — `rule:types/conversion`'s checked conversion, in exactly the place the member's
// own throw used to land. What is pinned here is the *bound*, and the bound is
// where it always was.
//
// The first edge is textual and PHP agrees with us on it: an escape needs both
// digits, so a trailing `%`, a one-digit tail and a non-hex pair are each left
// exactly as written rather than repaired or refused. `%41` on the other side
// of that line is the same three characters read as `A`.
array<string> $textual = ["a%", "a%4", "a%zzb", "%41", "%2541"];
foreach ($textual as string $row) {
    echo "[", $row, "] -> [", (Core\Uri::decodeComponent($row) as string), "]\n";
}

// The second edge is the one `rule:types/bytes` draws, and it is a real divergence from
// PHP: a PHP string is a byte string, so `rawurldecode("a%FFb")` there answers
// three bytes that are not text; a Novis `string` is UTF-8, so there is no
// value for this member to answer and it throws instead. Every family of
// invalid sequence is refused, not only the obviously-out-of-range byte —
// counted over the table, because a member validating the first octet alone
// still accepts a truncated sequence and a surrogate.
array<string> $octets = [
    "a%FFb",
    "%C3",
    "%ED%A0%80",
    "%C0%80",
    "%F5%80%80%80",
];
int $tried = 0;
int $refused = 0;
foreach ($octets as string $bad) {
    $tried = $tried + 1;
    try {
        // The call is bound before the `echo`, because `echo` writes its
        // earlier operands before a later one throws.
        var $read = (Core\Uri::decodeComponent($bad) as string);
        echo "accepted [", $read, "]\n";
    } catch (Throwable $notText) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
echo "tried ", $tried, " refused ", $refused, "\n";

// One of them with its message, since the refusal names which byte of the
// *decoded* octets is the problem rather than which character of the input.
// It is `bytes as string`'s message now, not this member's: the decoder has
// nothing left to refuse.
try {
    echo (Core\Uri::decodeComponent("a%FFb") as string), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $lone) {
    echo $lone->message, "\n";
}

// And the value on the accepting side of that edge, which is what makes it a
// bound rather than a blanket rule: a well-formed multi-byte sequence decodes
// to the one character it spells, and `%00` — the byte a C string could not
// hold — is an ordinary character here, so the refusal above is about UTF-8
// and nothing else. The NUL is measured rather than echoed, a raw NUL in the
// expected output being a byte no diff can show.
echo (Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%C3%A9") as string), " ", (Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%E2%82%AC") as string), " ", (Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%F0%9F%92%A9") as string), "\n";
echo Core\Str::length((Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%00") as string)), " ", Core\Str::length((Core\Uri::decodeComponent("") as string)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
[a%] -> [a%]
[a%4] -> [a%4]
[a%zzb] -> [a%zzb]
[%41] -> [A]
[%2541] -> [%41]
tried 5 refused 5
cannot convert `bytes` to `string`: not well-formed UTF-8 at byte 1
é € 💩
1 0
